// Project data, extracted from the original index.html.
// Single source of truth for the tile grid and the write up overlay.
const PROJECTS=[{"title": "Distributed ViT on CIFAR-10: Proving a Speedup, Then Explaining Why It Vanished", "meta": "Distributed Training &middot; Systems Measurement &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "98% &rarr; 57% scaling efficiency &middot; cause isolated &middot; 24 measured runs", "tags": ["PyTorch", "DDP", "FSDP", "NCCL", "Mixed Precision", "torch.compile", "Vision Transformer", "CUDA", "Benchmarking", "Apple MPS"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">2x Tesla T4, NCCL &nbsp;|&nbsp; DDP in fp32: 1.96x at 98% efficiency &nbsp;|&nbsp; identical config in fp16: 1.15x at 57% &nbsp;|&nbsp; cause isolated to a 4 vCPU host &nbsp;|&nbsp; 24 measured runs across 3 hardware groups</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Almost every distributed training result you read is unfalsifiable. A repository reports a 1.9x speedup on two GPUs and there is no way to tell whether the two rank job computed the same gradient as the one rank job, whether the baseline was tuned as carefully as the treatment, or whether the number came from a machine doing something else at the time. A speedup you cannot verify is not a result, it is a claim. This project was built so that every claim it makes is checkable, and the most interesting thing it found is a regression in its own scaling.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>What Gets Measured, and Against What</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three hardware groups: an Apple M5 laptop with one GPU, multi process gloo over that laptop's 10 CPU cores, and 2x Tesla T4 on Kaggle with NCCL. Speedup is only ever computed inside one group, because a ratio between a T4 and an M5 is arithmetic, not a measurement. A rank scaling baseline must differ from its run in exactly one property: the rank count. Precision, compilation, gradient accumulation, dataloader worker count, epoch count and dataset fraction all have to match, and the reporting layer enforces that rather than trusting the author. That constraint was added after the dashboard was caught dividing a real data run by a synthetic data baseline, which silently reported 1.11x where the matched comparison is 1.15x.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Model and Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            A vision transformer written out rather than imported, 1,806,538 parameters, patch size 4 on 32&times;32 inputs giving 64 tokens plus a class token. Small on purpose: the model must not be the bottleneck in a throughput measurement, and at this size the gradient payload for each all reduce is about 7 MB, which matters later. CIFAR-10, 50,000 train and 10,000 test, 30 epochs, no pretraining.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            The canonical dataset host was unreachable from the development network, failing with an SSL EOF before any payload arrived. The fallback deliberately does not rebuild the original pickle layout, because those files carry published md5 sums a reconstruction cannot match, and the only way to make that path appear to work would be to disable torchvision's integrity checking. Silencing a checksum to make a download look successful is how results stop being trustworthy. The mirror writes its own format instead, and every run artifact records which source it used.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Configuration</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Architecture</td><td>ViT, dim 192, depth 6, heads 3, MLP ratio 2.0</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Regularization</td><td>Stochastic depth 0.1, label smoothing 0.1, grad clip 1.0</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Optimizer</td><td>AdamW, betas (0.9, 0.95), no decay on norms, biases, class token, position embedding</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Schedule</td><td>Linear warmup 2 epochs, then cosine, computed per step</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Learning rate</td><td>1e-3, scaled linearly by world size</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Batch size</td><td>128 per rank</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Precision</td><td>Selected from compute capability, not from <code>is_bf16_supported</code></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Runtime</td><td>torch 2.10.0+cu128, NCCL on CUDA, gloo on CPU, FileStore rendezvous on macOS</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>Correctness Before Speed</h4>\n          <p>\n            DDP averages gradients across ranks, so two ranks each holding batch B must produce the same gradient as one process holding batch 2B. If that identity does not hold, every throughput number in the project describes a system training a different model than its own baseline, and the comparison is void. The suite spawns a real gloo process group and checks it to <code>rtol=1e-4</code>. It ships with a second test that deliberately computes the wrong thing, because a correctness test that cannot fail is decoration rather than evidence.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/dvit_03.png\" alt=\"Terminal showing the DDP gradient equivalence test and its inverse guard both passing\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 1. THE GRADIENT EQUIVALENCE TEST AND ITS INVERSE GUARD, RUN IN A REAL PROCESS GROUP. THE DASHBOARD IS THEN REGENERATED FROM 24 RUN ARTIFACTS</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Scaling on 2x Tesla T4</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Ranks</th><th>Strategy</th><th>Precision</th><th>img/s</th><th>Rank speedup</th><th>Peak MB</th><th>Top-1</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws1-fp32</code></td><td>1</td><td>single</td><td>fp32</td><td>1,853</td><td>baseline</td><td>553</td><td>80.70%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws1-amp</code></td><td>1</td><td>single</td><td>fp16</td><td>3,810</td><td>baseline</td><td>347</td><td>80.52%</td></tr>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-fp32</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp32</td><td><strong>3,637</strong></td><td><strong>1.96x (98%)</strong></td><td>561</td><td>80.01%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-amp</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp16</td><td>4,376</td><td>1.15x (57%)</td><td>354</td><td>79.71%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-amp-compiled</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp16 + compile</td><td>4,706</td><td>no baseline</td><td>345</td><td>80.09%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-amp-ga2</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp16, accum 2</td><td>4,393</td><td>no baseline</td><td>361</td><td>80.15%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-fsdp-amp</code></td><td>2</td><td>FSDP</td><td>fp16</td><td>2,884</td><td>0.76x</td><td>338</td><td>79.41%</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>\n            Three predictions were written down before this ran, so they could be scored instead of retrofitted. FSDP was predicted to lose, and it does: 2,884 img/s against DDP's 4,376, buying 5% of peak memory for 34% of throughput. At 1,806,538 parameters there is almost nothing to shard. Mixed precision was predicted to be a large win, and it is: 2.06x at one rank with peak memory falling from 553 MB to 347 MB.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>The Prediction That Was Wrong</h4>\n          <p>\n            The third prediction said DDP would land near 1.8x rather than 2x, reasoning that a small model makes the all reduce expensive relative to compute. That is backwards. A small model means <em>small gradient tensors</em>, about 7 MB, which DDP overlaps with the backward pass almost entirely. In fp32 it scales at 1.96x, 98% efficiency.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            The same DDP configuration in fp16 scales at 1.15x, 57% efficiency. Identical hardware, identical model, identical communication volume. The only thing that changed is that each GPU now computes roughly twice as fast. Optimising one layer moved the bottleneck rather than shortening the job, and scaling efficiency turned out not to be a property of the cluster at all.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Isolating the Cause</h4>\n          <p>\n            Two suspects: the NCCL all reduce, and the input pipeline. Raising the dataloader worker count and watching what happens would produce a correlation, not a cause. Instead a synthetic input mode serves pre normalised tensors from a small in memory pool, removing decode, augmentation and worker processes entirely, so two ranks against one rank under it is compute plus communication with nothing else in the measurement.\n          </p>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Input path</th><th>1 rank</th><th>2 ranks</th><th>Speedup</th><th>Efficiency</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Synthetic, no dataloader</strong></td><td>3,956</td><td><strong>7,580</strong></td><td><strong>1.92x</strong></td><td><strong>96%</strong></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Real data, 2 workers per rank</td><td>3,462</td><td>4,073</td><td>1.18x</td><td>59%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Real data, 8 workers per rank</td><td>3,599</td><td>3,996</td><td>1.11x</td><td>56%</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>\n            The collective is innocent. With no dataloader the same configuration scales at 1.92x, 96% efficiency. With real data, throughput never passes about 4,073 img/s no matter how many GPUs or workers are thrown at it. The artifacts record <code>cpu_cores: 4</code>. One T4 in fp16 already consumes 3,956 img/s of synthetic input, so 4 vCPUs cannot feed even one GPU comfortably, let alone two. The second T4 is not slow, it is unfed.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            Raising workers from 2 to 8 per rank made it <em>worse</em>, 3,996 against 4,073, because 16 worker processes on 4 cores buy context switches and nothing else. Note that the synthetic runs also index a small pool, so their 96% is an upper bound on what fixing the input path could achieve, not a promise.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/dvit_01.png\" alt=\"Median throughput for every measured run across three hardware groups\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 2. MEDIAN THROUGHPUT ACROSS ALL MEASURED RUNS. SYNTHETIC INPUT RUNS ARE MARKED, BECAUSE THE FASTEST BAR IS AN INSTRUMENT READING AND NOT A TRAINING RESULT</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Precision Advice Does Not Survive a Backend Change</h4>\n          <p>\n            On the T4, enabling mixed precision is a 2.06x win. On Apple silicon the identical flag is a regression: 726 img/s against 1,061 in fp32, a 32% slowdown, because eager mode pays for a cast around every operator and the hardware is already efficient at fp32. Adding <code>torch.compile</code> to that same fp16 configuration reaches 2,361 img/s, 2.23x the fp32 baseline, once the casts are fused into the graph. Wall clock for the full 30 epoch run falls from 24 minutes to 11.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            A related trap: on a T4, <code>torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported()</code> returns True. The card is sm_75 and has no hardware bf16, so that True reflects emulation. Trusting it selected an emulated path for every mixed precision run, and torch inductor quietly logged that the card does not support bfloat16 natively while the benchmark carried on reporting the result as mixed precision. Precision is now selected from compute capability, and every artifact records the dtype that was actually used.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Accuracy as the Control</h4>\n          <p>\n            Across all seven T4 configurations, top-1 spans 79.41% to 80.70%, and the three Apple silicon runs land within 0.16 points of each other. That is the control, not a footnote. These are throughput changes, so accuracy should barely move. If mixed precision or compilation had shifted it materially, the correct output would have been a bug report rather than a results table.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/dvit_02.png\" alt=\"Test accuracy by epoch for the full length runs, curves nearly overlapping\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 3. TEST ACCURACY BY EPOCH. THE CURVES OVERLAY BECAUSE PRECISION AND COMPILATION CHANGE THROUGHPUT, NOT LEARNING. SHORT AND SYNTHETIC RUNS ARE EXCLUDED FROM THIS VIEW BY THE REPORTING LAYER</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Nine Bugs, Kept On Purpose</h4>\n          <p>\n            The defect log is the most reusable output of the build, so it ships in the repository rather than being tidied away. Four of the nine came from an API answering a slightly different question than the one being asked: <code>is_bf16_supported</code> counting emulation, torchvision's md5 check rejecting a legitimate mirror, MPS reporting instantaneous memory where a peak was wanted, and <code>sys.argv[0]</code> producing a command that could not reproduce its own run. One was a hang rather than a crash: the default TCP rendezvous is blocked on some hosts and the job simply waits, which reads exactly like slow training and costs hours before anyone suspects the launcher. Three were wrong numbers on the dashboard, all found by pointing a screen recorder at a page nobody had read closely.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            That last category is why the honesty rule is now enforced rather than promised. A gate extracts every number carrying a measurement unit from the documentation and fails if it does not round, at the precision it was written with, to an artifact value or to a ratio between two artifact values on the same hardware. It found five unsupported claims the first time it ran.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Operational Reading</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              The headline is not the 1.96x. It is that the same cluster, model and communication volume delivered 98% efficiency in fp32 and 57% in fp16, and that the difference had nothing to do with the distributed layer. Anyone tuning NCCL in response to that drop would have spent a week in the wrong subsystem. Scaling efficiency is a property of the ratio between accelerator speed and host speed, and optimising the accelerator is what breaks it. The measurement that settles it took six short runs and one flag.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "ZTF Transient Pipeline: Real Time Astronomical Alert Classification and Warehouse", "meta": "Streaming ML &middot; Analytics Engineering &middot; Jul 2026", "badge": "27,378 real ZTF alerts &middot; multimodal CNN &middot; dbt: 8 models, 21 tests", "tags": ["Python", "Apache Kafka", "Avro", "TensorFlow", "Multimodal CNN", "dbt", "DuckDB", "Apache Airflow", "FastAPI", "Astropy"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">27,378 real Avro alerts (ZTF, Jul 20 2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; 500 scored end to end &nbsp;|&nbsp; dbt: 8 models, 21 tests, 32 checks passing &nbsp;|&nbsp; two plane architecture &nbsp;|&nbsp; portable DuckDB to BigQuery / Snowflake</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>Time domain astronomy has an inversion problem: the telescope produces far more data than any human can look at, and the overwhelming majority of it is noise. A survey astronomer or alert broker operator needs high purity transient candidates surfaced quickly, and needs to trust that the nightly numbers behind them are correct. That is two distinct jobs: a fast, accurate model at the front, and a tested, governed data layer behind it. I built ZTF Transient Pipeline to do both in one system, which also happens to map cleanly onto the three roles I am applying for: ML engineer, data engineer, and analytics engineer.</p>\n\n          <h4>The Sequel to ShopFloor</h4>\n          <p>This project was designed deliberately to close the three honest gaps I named in ShopFloor AI. Simulated Kafka becomes real Avro alert data flowing over a real Kafka broker. A synthetic benchmark dataset becomes a genuine public survey stream. And a single node app gains a real warehouse, a dbt transformation layer, and drift monitoring. It is the same instinct, scaled up to production shaped infrastructure.</p>\n\n          <h4>The Data</h4>\n          <p>The Zwicky Transient Facility (ZTF) streams over a million optical alerts per night as Avro packets over Kafka. Each packet embeds three small image cutouts (science, template, and difference) plus a table of measured features. The pipeline downloads a night of real archived alerts and replays them through its own Dockerized Kafka broker: the payloads are genuine ZTF data, only the timing is replayed. That is stated plainly in the README, because it is real data flowing through a real broker without needing broker whitelisting on the critical path.</p>\n\n          <h4>Streaming ML Plane</h4>\n          <p>Alerts are deserialized with fastavro, with Astropy handling the astronomical coordinate and time conventions. A multimodal classifier in TensorFlow and Keras scores each alert: a convolutional branch reads the three image cutouts, a tabular branch reads the measured features, and the two are fused to predict whether an alert is a real transient or an artifact, then a coarse class. Rather than invent an accuracy target, the model is framed against BTSbot, a published survey broker classifier that scores detections from the same image plus feature inputs. A FastAPI service exposes the scorer, and a streaming consumer writes predictions and alert metadata into the warehouse.</p>\n\n          <h4>Analytics Engineering Plane</h4>\n          <p>This is the half that earns the SQL and analytics engineering story. A dbt project transforms the raw landed alerts through three layers: staging models that clean and type the raw stream, intermediate models that assemble per object lightcurves using window functions, and mart models including an incremental daily alert summary and a model monitoring rollup. The project ships with 21 data quality tests, source freshness checks, and generated lineage docs. It defaults to DuckDB so anyone can run it with zero cloud setup, and the same models are portable to BigQuery and Snowflake with only a profile change.</p>\n\n          <h4>Orchestration and Monitoring</h4>\n          <p>An Apache Airflow DAG runs the full nightly batch end to end: download, deserialize, load, transform, and monitor. A Population Stability Index drift monitor watches the prediction distribution over time, so a shift in what the model is seeing surfaces as a number rather than a surprise. Continuous integration through GitHub Actions runs the test suite and a dbt build on every push, and a Codespaces devcontainer lets the whole thing spin up in the browser.</p>\n\n          <h4>Proven on Real Data</h4>\n          <p>The pipeline was run against a genuine night of the survey: 27,378 Avro alert packets extracted from July 20, 2026, with 500 alerts loaded, scored, and rebuilt through the warehouse. A full <code>dbt build</code> returned 8 models and 21 tests with 32 checks passing and zero errors, on real data rather than a seed sample. The repository is public at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/ztf-transient-pipeline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/ztf-transient-pipeline</a>.</p>\n\n          <h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Replay, not live consumption.</strong> Nightly archive replay is real data, but true live streaming from the survey broker needs an IP whitelist, kept as an optional upgrade rather than a dependency.</li>\n            <li><strong>Coarse labels.</strong> The classifier demonstrates the pipeline, not a science grade taxonomy. It is a systems project first.</li>\n            <li><strong>Trial scale compute.</strong> Everything runs single node under Docker Compose, not at production survey volume.</li>\n            <li><strong>Validation against broker labels.</strong> No spectroscopic confirmation; the reference labels are themselves model outputs, so the benchmark is relative rather than ground truth.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">One repository, three role narratives. For an ML engineer, a multimodal image plus tabular classifier benchmarked against published work. For a data engineer, a real Avro over Kafka ingestion pipeline orchestrated nightly with Airflow. For an analytics engineer, a dbt transformation layer with tests, freshness, lineage, and warehouse portability. The unifying point is that the model is only one component, and the value lives in the tested, monitored, reproducible system around it.</p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "Groundwork: RAG Evaluation, Guardrails, and an LLM Gateway on Real Data", "meta": "LLM Platform Engineering &middot; RAG Evaluation &middot; Aug 2026", "badge": "recall@5 0.921 &middot; ECE 0.045 &middot; injection recall 0.884 unseen &middot; 113 tests, CI green", "tags": ["Python", "RAG", "LLM Gateway", "Guardrails", "Calibration (ECE)", "Prompt Injection", "BM25", "FastAPI", "Docker", "Kubernetes", "Responsible AI", "CI/CD"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">recall@5 0.921 &nbsp;|&nbsp; ECE 0.045 &nbsp;|&nbsp; injection recall 0.884 on an unseen attack corpus &nbsp;|&nbsp; correctly declines 96.2% of unanswerable questions &nbsp;|&nbsp; 11,873 questions evaluated per run &nbsp;|&nbsp; 113 tests, CI green</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>The hard part of shipping an LLM feature is not getting a good answer once. It is knowing whether the answer is good, knowing when the system should refuse, proving the guardrails do not reject a tenth of your legitimate traffic, and being able to say what a change cost you. Groundwork is those four things built around a small retrieval augmented generation system, so they have something concrete to be true about.</p>\n<p><strong>Every number in the project is measured on real, public, human labelled data.</strong> No corpus was written for the demo, no gold answers were authored to be easy, and no attack was invented to be caught. A fetch script downloads five datasets and records a SHA-256 for each in a manifest. It runs with no API key at all, on a deterministic offline reader, so every figure below is reproducible on a laptop for free.</p>\n\n<h4>Four Concerns, One Codebase</h4>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>What is implemented</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>LLM and RAG evaluation</td><td>Retrieval metrics, SQuAD official answer metrics, abstention scored as a classifier, confidence calibration with reliability diagrams, a three way retrieval ablation, and a release gate that exits non zero</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Guardrails</td><td>Input and output rails, each scored on a published labelled attack corpus and again on an attack corpus it has never seen</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Secure deployment</td><td>Hashed API keys with constant time comparison, a tamper evident audit hash chain, PII redaction measured per entity type, a threat model, and a hardened container</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LLM gateway</td><td>One chokepoint for every model call: auth, tier routing, two layer cache, per tenant budgets and rate limits, retries, graceful degradation, full cost accounting</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The Data</h4>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Role</th><th>Size</th><th>Licence</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>SQuAD v2.0 dev</td><td>Corpus of 1,204 Wikipedia paragraphs, 11,873 questions, gold answer spans, and 5,945 genuinely unanswerable questions</td><td>4.2 MB</td><td>CC BY-SA 4.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>deepset/prompt-injections</td><td>Injection rail precision and recall</td><td>662</td><td>CC BY 4.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>jackhhao/jailbreak-classification</td><td>Jailbreak rail</td><td>1,306</td><td>See card</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Lakera/gandalf_ignore_instructions</td><td>Held out attacks, never trained on</td><td>1,000</td><td>MIT</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>ai4privacy/pii-masking-200k</td><td>PII redaction recall per entity type</td><td>1,000</td><td>See card</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">Four production concerns that usually get skipped, implemented and measured rather than asserted: RAG evaluation with a gate that can fail a build, guardrails with published false positive rates on corpora they have never seen, an LLM gateway that meters every call, and a documented security posture. The thread running through all of it is that <strong>the measurement changed the system.</strong> The full public write-up and code are at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/Groundwork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/Groundwork</a>.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "Replicating BEIR: Reproducing a Retrieval Benchmark and Finding a Silent Truncation Bug", "meta": "Information Retrieval &middot; Reproducibility &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "6 of 6 published cells reproduced &middot; 4 exact &middot; silent truncation bug found and fixed", "tags": ["Python", "Information Retrieval", "BEIR", "BM25", "Dense Retrieval", "sentence-transformers", "DPR", "TAS-B", "pytrec_eval", "Reciprocal Rank Fusion", "Reproducibility"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">6 of 6 published cells reproduced &nbsp;|&nbsp; 4 exact to three decimals &nbsp;|&nbsp; DPR on SciFact recovered from 0.260 to a published 0.318 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 12 minutes on a laptop, no GPU &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to an artifact in runs/</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>Reading a paper tells you what the authors claim. Rebuilding it tells you whether the claim survives contact with a different machine, a different implementation and a fresh set of defaults. I rebuilt the central experiment from <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08663\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BEIR</a> (Thakur et al., NeurIPS 2021 Datasets and Benchmarks) from scratch: the claim that dense retrievers trained on MS MARCO lose to a plain bag of words baseline once you move them to a domain they were not trained on.</p>\n<p>The useful part of a replication is not the agreement. It is the one cell that disagrees, because chasing it is the only way to learn something the paper did not tell you.</p>\n\n<h4>What Reproduced</h4>\n<p>Two scientific datasets from the benchmark, chosen because they are the smallest and therefore fit on a laptop: SciFact (5,183 documents, 300 queries) and NFCorpus (3,633 documents, 323 queries). Both counts match the paper exactly. Metric is nDCG@10, scored with pytrec_eval, the same trec_eval bindings the authors report with.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>SciFact published</th><th>SciFact mine</th><th>NFCorpus published</th><th>NFCorpus mine</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>BM25</td><td>0.665</td><td>0.677</td><td>0.325</td><td>0.320</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TAS-B</td><td>0.643</td><td><strong>0.643</strong></td><td>0.319</td><td><strong>0.319</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DPR</td><td>0.318</td><td><strong>0.318</strong></td><td>0.189</td><td>0.188</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>bge-small (2023, not in the paper)</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.713</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.343</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hybrid RRF (not in the paper)</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.687</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.341</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The paper holds for the models the paper tested. It does not survive a 2023 embedder: bge-small-en-v1.5, at 33M parameters, beats BM25 on both datasets. The authors were right in 2021 and the gap they identified has since closed. Reciprocal rank fusion over BM25 and TAS-B also beat both of its own inputs on both datasets, and took the best Recall@100 on SciFact at 0.948.</p>\n\n<h4>The Cell That Disagreed</h4>\n<p>DPR on SciFact came in at 0.260 against a published 0.318. Not a rounding difference. TAS-B had already reproduced exactly on both datasets, so the loader, the metrics and the scoring were not at fault, which left something specific to how DPR sees a document.</p>\n<p>The cause is a default. The sentence-transformers port of the DPR context encoder ships with <code>max_seq_length = 100</code>, so every abstract was cut off after roughly its first paragraph. No error, no warning, no sign of it in the output. The tell was that searching with <strong>only the title</strong> scored 0.261 against title plus full text at 0.260. That only makes sense if the body was being discarded.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Context encoder token limit</th><th>nDCG@10</th><th>Recall@100</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>100 (the shipped default)</td><td>0.276</td><td>0.601</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>256</td><td>0.298</td><td>0.687</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>350</td><td>0.315</td><td>0.710</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>512</td><td><strong>0.318</strong></td><td><strong>0.727</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>published</td><td>0.318</td><td>0.727</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The bug was invisible on NFCorpus, which reproduced either way. NFCorpus carries 38.2 judged documents per query against SciFact’s 1.1, so it absorbs ranking damage that SciFact exposes. A single dataset would have hidden this entirely, which is the practical lesson: a benchmark with one dataset is a benchmark that cannot tell you when you are wrong.</p>\n\n<h4>What Is Not Identical To The Paper</h4>\n<p>Every reproduction deviates somewhere. Stating the deviations is the difference between a replication and a claim.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Deviation</th><th>Why, and what it costs</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>BM25 is not Anserini</td><td>The paper scores BM25 with Anserini, which is Lucene. That needs a Java runtime the machine did not have, so BM25 is reimplemented in Python with the same k1 of 0.9 and b of 0.4, the same Porter stemming and the same 33 word Lucene English stopword list, but one concatenated field instead of two and exact rather than quantised document lengths. This is the likely source of the only two inexact cells, +0.012 and -0.005.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Plain rather than capped recall</td><td>The paper reports capped Recall@100. The two agree for every SciFact query but not for every NFCorpus query.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>bge-small is an addition</td><td>A 2023 model the paper could not have tested, included to measure how far the field moved, and labelled as not in the paper wherever it appears.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hybrid RRF is an addition</td><td>Reciprocal rank fusion with k of 60, from Cormack et al. 2009.</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">That I can take a published result, rebuild it independently, and say precisely where my numbers agree and where they do not. The valuable output was not the six matching cells; it was the one that did not match, and the fact that chasing it surfaced a <strong>library default that silently discards most of every document</strong> with no error and no warning. That failure mode is not specific to this paper. It is waiting in any retrieval system whose chunks are longer than an embedding model’s configured limit, which is most of them. Every number here traces to a command that was run and a JSON artifact committed under <code>runs/</code>. Code at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/beir-replication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/beir-replication</a>.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "JMA Wireless: Standardizing Five Factory KPIs and Locating the Real Constraint", "meta": "Lean Six Sigma &middot; Analytics Engineering &middot; Jan to May 2026", "badge": "project lead, 4 person team &middot; 31 GB JSON to under 5 MB &middot; ~$355K projected annualized opportunity", "tags": ["DMAIC", "Power BI", "DAX", "Python", "Pandas", "openpyxl", "ETL", "Minitab", "SIPOC", "Pugh Matrix", "FMEA", "Control Plan"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">5 KPIs standardized across 2 lines &nbsp;|&nbsp; 31 GB of nested PLC JSON reduced to under 5 MB &nbsp;|&nbsp; automated line at 97.3% uptime but 46.3% schedule attainment &nbsp;|&nbsp; ~$355K projected annualized opportunity &nbsp;|&nbsp; 15 week DMAIC engagement</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>JMA Wireless builds cell tower transmitters and active 5G infrastructure in Liverpool, New York. Its Jumper Department produces the radio frequency jumper cables that connect transmitters to the antennas on towers and small cells: functionally simple parts that are precision sensitive, since every connector must be terminated to tight torque and electrical specification and every cable must pass continuity, return loss, and visual inspection before it ships.</p>\n<p>The department runs two parallel lines with completely different economics. Jumper 1.0 is manual, staffed by eight operators across two ten hour shifts. Jumper 2.0 is a fully automated cell with PLC level data capture. Before this project there was no way to compare them, because terms like Uptime and Schedule Attainment meant different things to different functions. Leadership could not draw a clean line from what happened on the floor on Tuesday to the financial plan.</p>\n\n<h4>What I Owned</h4>\n<p>I was Project Lead on a four person Whitman School team running a full DMAIC engagement from January to May 2026, sponsored by JMA’s SVP of Global Operations with a named project champion and weekly on site access. I authored the charter, SIPOC, and CTQC tree, ran the KPI definition interviews, built the Python pipeline that made the automated line’s data usable, and wrote the Control Plan that governs the dashboard after handover.</p>\n\n<h4>The Method</h4>\n<p>\n  DMAIC as actually run, including the loops that fired. Two of them mattered: a root cause that did not survive validation went back for re analysis, and a target that the data showed was unreachable went back to be revalidated rather than left red on a dashboard forever.\n</p>\n\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/jma_02.jpg\" alt=\"DMAIC project flow from problem identification through KPI definition, baseline, root cause analysis, solution selection, dashboard build, control plan and monitoring\">\n  <figcaption>FIGURE 1. THE DMAIC PATH RUN ON THIS ENGAGEMENT, INCLUDING THE RE ANALYSIS AND TARGET VALIDATION LOOPS</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h4>Standardizing the KPIs</h4>\n<p>\n  The single most important Define deliverable was a written, sponsor approved definition document. Five KPIs, each with one agreed formula and one agreed target, confirmed by the sponsor before anything was built.\n</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>KPI</th><th>Formula</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Parts per Labor Hour</td><td>Total good parts / total labor hours</td><td>&ge; 50</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Uptime</td><td>Runtime / (runtime + downtime)</td><td>&ge; 85%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>First Time Throughput</td><td>Good parts / total parts tested</td><td>&ge; 95%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Schedule Attainment</td><td>Actual parts / weekly target</td><td>&ge; 100%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Culture and Engagement</td><td>Pulse score (60%) + weekly active dashboard users (40%)</td><td>Index &ge; 75</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>\n  The Culture and Engagement metric is the unusual one. Both JMA and Whitman treat culture as a real lean variable, but standard manufacturing data never captures it, so I specified a five question two minute pulse survey plus a passive count of distinct named users who open the dashboard at least twice a week.\n</p>\n\n<h4>The Data Engineering Problem</h4>\n<p>\n  The automated cell publishes one JSON record per test event, and each record nests an array of tests, each nesting an array of measurements, each nesting an array of channel readings. Two raw datasets of 809 and 1,000 records expanded to more than 31 GB when loaded straight into Power BI. That is unusable twice over: Power BI expands the nesting on every visual that touches the data, and nobody with less than 64 GB of RAM can open the file at all.\n</p>\n<p>\n  I built a Python pipeline with pandas and openpyxl that walks the nesting with explicit <code>json_normalize</code> record paths and writes one flat row per test event. It casts timestamps to datetime, channel readings to float, and pass and fail to boolean, so Power Query never has to coerce anything, and it computes cycle time in seconds as a direct input to the Uptime and Parts per Labor Hour measures. The output is under 5 MB and loads instantly.\n</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Extract</td><td>Load raw test report JSON</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Parse</td><td>Walk the nesting with explicit <code>json_normalize</code> record paths</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Type cast</td><td>datetime64, float64, boolean, so Power BI needs no coercion</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Calculate</td><td>Derive cycle time in seconds from start and end timestamps</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Load</td><td>Write a Power BI ready workbook via openpyxl</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Baseline: What the Standardized Numbers Showed</h4>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>KPI</th><th>Jumper 1.0 (manual)</th><th>Jumper 2.0 (automated)</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>First Time Throughput</td><td>87.9%</td><td>92.5%</td><td>95%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Uptime</td><td>31.3%</td><td><strong>97.3%</strong></td><td>85%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Schedule Attainment</td><td>139.7%</td><td><strong>46.3%</strong></td><td>100%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Parts per Labor Hour</td><td>34.91</td><td>7.94</td><td>50</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>\n  Parts per Labor Hour is the one number on that table that should not be read straight across. Both lines use the same scheduled labor hours denominator so the comparison is consistent, but the automated cell only needs a supervising technician for part of a shift. Measured on engaged technician minutes it is roughly an order of magnitude higher, so the dashboard exposes both that figure and a cell specific cycles per engaged hour measure rather than letting 7.94 stand as a verdict.\n</p>\n\n<h4>The Finding That Mattered</h4>\n<p>\n  The automated cell held <strong>97.3% uptime</strong> and still delivered only <strong>46.3% of its weekly target</strong>. Those two numbers cannot both be a machine problem. A cell that is running almost all of the time and still missing plan by more than half is not equipment limited, it is input limited. The binding constraint was what entered the cell, not the cell itself.\n</p>\n<p>\n  The manual line was quietly absorbing the shortfall at 139.7% of its own target, which looks like heroics and reads well on a weekly report, but it depresses labor productivity, strains the operator pool, and masks the real demand signal going back to planning. Standardizing the definitions is what made the pair of numbers comparable enough for the pattern to be visible at all.\n</p>\n<p>\n  A fishbone on that gap put the leading causes in Method, Material, and Measurement: kitting batches sized for the manual line, connector subassemblies arriving batched rather than continuously, and a weekly target set at commissioning that had never been revisited against demonstrated capability.\n</p>\n\n<h4>Where Quality and Time Actually Go</h4>\n<p>\n  Two Paretos carried the Analyze phase. On quality, torque and return loss together account for roughly three quarters of all first time failures, which points at tooling and torque training on one side and connector seating on the other, not at a diffuse quality problem.\n</p>\n\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/jma_01.jpg\" alt=\"Pareto chart of first time throughput failure modes on Jumper 1.0: torque out of spec 41 percent, return loss 33 percent, visual reject 14 percent, continuity fail 8 percent, other 4 percent\">\n  <figcaption>FIGURE 2. FTT FAILURE MODES ON JUMPER 1.0. TORQUE AND RETURN LOSS ARE 74% OF ALL FIRST TIME FAILURES</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>\n  On time, the manual line loses more than a third of its scheduled hours waiting for material and another quarter to changeover, which is the same upstream flow problem that starves the automated cell, showing up in a different shape.\n</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Jumper 1.0 downtime category</th><th>Share</th><th>Typical event</th><th>Per week</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Material wait (cable, connectors)</td><td><strong>38%</strong></td><td>18 min</td><td>~24</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Setup and changeover</td><td>27%</td><td>32 min</td><td>~9</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Tool calibration and maintenance</td><td>19%</td><td>22 min</td><td>~9</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Operator absent or break overrun</td><td>12%</td><td>11 min</td><td>~11</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Other</td><td>4%</td><td>mixed</td><td>mixed</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Recommendations, and the One I Piloted</h4>\n<p>\n  Three recommendations came out of Improve, selected against six candidates with a Pugh matrix and sequenced on a benefit and effort grid: a fifteen minute daily standup held at a wall mounted screen showing the dashboard, a two bin continuous kitting feed so the automated cell is never starved of inputs, and a reset of the cell’s weekly target from the number set at commissioning to demonstrated capability, with a documented ramp as kitting improves.\n</p>\n<p>\n  The third one is worth defending because it looks like moving the goalposts. A target the cell cannot currently hit makes the dashboard permanently red, and a permanently red dashboard is one people stop reading. Resetting it to roughly 1,150 cables per week and ramping by about 200 per week as the kitting fix lands means the team can see progress it is actually making.\n</p>\n<p>\n  I piloted the standup for two weeks on the manual line first shift and measured it rather than asserting it worked. Median time from a KPI going red to a named owner taking the first action fell from about <strong>4 hours to about 25 minutes</strong>, because the standup surfaced the red status instead of a supervisor finding it on a walk. Operator self reported familiarity with the five KPIs rose from <strong>2.4 to 4.1</strong> on a five point scale.\n</p>\n\n<h4>What the Dollar Figure Rests On</h4>\n<p>\n  The headline number is a projection, not money in the bank, and it is worth being precise about that. Closing the automated line’s schedule attainment gap to plan recovers roughly <strong>27,000 cables per year</strong> with no added labor. At an assumed <strong>$12 contribution margin per cable</strong> that is about $324,000 a year. Retiring the manual weekly reporting cycle frees about <strong>48 analyst hours per week</strong>, roughly $31,200 a year at an assumed <strong>$12.50 fully loaded hourly rate</strong>. Together, about $355,000 of recurring annual opportunity.\n</p>\n<p>\n  Both of those rates are conservative assumptions the team adopted pending confirmation from JMA Finance, and the report states them in an assumptions table precisely so any figure can be revised without re deriving the analysis. The cable recovery is a projection from demonstrated capability, not an observed post implementation result.\n</p>\n\n<h4>What I Would Do Differently</h4>\n<p>\n  The Culture and Engagement metric was scoped late and is the one KPI with no baseline value; the instrument is built and wired into the dashboard but the first administration slipped past the engagement. I also did not write unit tests for the Python pipeline. It produces correct output today, but the only thing standing between a schema change upstream and a silently wrong dashboard is an analyst noticing, and a small set of assertions would have removed that dependency before handover.\n</p>\n\n<h4>Sustainment</h4>\n<p>\n  The dashboard ships with a Control Plan rather than a handshake: a named refresh owner and cadence, an FMEA on the dashboard itself covering the ways a stale or broken feed could mislead a supervisor, and a tiered escalation framework that triggers on red KPI status so a red light has a defined next action instead of being ambient.\n</p>\n\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<div class=\"callout\">\n  <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">Operations analytics where the engineering and the judgement both had to be right. The pipeline work was real (31 GB of nested PLC JSON is not a dataset you can wish into Power BI), but the finding that mattered came from definitions, not code: once Uptime and Schedule Attainment meant the same thing on both lines, <strong>97.3% uptime against 46.3% attainment</strong> immediately located the constraint upstream of the machine everyone assumed was the problem. It also demonstrates measuring your own intervention, since the standup pilot was tested and reported rather than claimed, and labelling a projection as a projection when a bigger number was available for the taking.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "ShopFloor AI: Predictive Maintenance and RAG for the Factory Floor", "meta": "MLOps &middot; Predictive Maintenance &middot; Jul 2026", "badge": "0.85 F1 &middot; 0.96 AUC &middot; 7-service deployed stack", "tags": ["Python", "Scikit learn", "Gradient Boosting", "MLflow", "Apache Kafka", "Apache Airflow", "Docker Compose", "FastAPI", "Streamlit", "LangChain", "ChromaDB", "RAG"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Gradient Boosting F1 = 0.85 &nbsp;|&nbsp; AUC-ROC = 0.96 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3 models tracked in MLflow &nbsp;|&nbsp; 16 engineered features &nbsp;|&nbsp; 7-service Docker Compose stack &nbsp;|&nbsp; AI4I 2020 (UCI, CC BY 4.0)</div>\n\n          <h4>Overview</h4>\n          <p>ShopFloor AI is an end to end manufacturing intelligence platform that predicts machine failures from sensor data, streams real time production KPIs through Apache Kafka, and answers natural language questions over maintenance documentation using retrieval augmented generation. It combines classical ML for failure classification, real time streaming for KPI monitoring, and LLM powered document retrieval, wired together as seven containerized services rather than a notebook. Built on the AI4I 2020 Predictive Maintenance Dataset (UCI ML Repository, CC BY 4.0).</p>\n\n          <h4>Tech Stack</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Technology</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Streaming</td><td>Apache Kafka</td><td>Real time sensor ingestion and anomaly alerting</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>ML tracking</td><td>MLflow</td><td>Experiment logging, model comparison, registry</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Orchestration</td><td>Apache Airflow</td><td>Scheduled retraining with data quality gates</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>RAG</td><td>LangChain + ChromaDB + OpenAI API</td><td>Natural language Q&amp;A over SOPs</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>API</td><td>FastAPI</td><td>REST endpoints for predictions and queries</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Streamlit + Plotly</td><td>KPI monitoring, failure predictor, chat, drift</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Docker Compose</td><td>7 containerized microservices</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>ML</td><td>scikit-learn</td><td>Gradient Boosting / Random Forest classifiers</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>How It Works</h4>\n          <p>A Streamlit dashboard calls a FastAPI backend, which fronts three subsystems: an MLflow model registry for predictions, a ChromaDB vector store queried through LangChain and the OpenAI API for document Q&amp;A, and a Kafka broker carrying the live sensor stream. A producer replays sensor data onto the topic and a consumer computes rolling KPIs and raises anomaly alerts. An Airflow DAG retrains the model nightly behind data quality gates.</p>\n          <pre>Streamlit dashboard\n     |  HTTP\nFastAPI backend  -->  /predict  ·  /ask  ·  /health\n     |-- MLflow model registry\n     |-- ChromaDB vector DB  -->  LangChain + OpenAI  (RAG)\n     +-- Kafka broker  -->  producer (sensor stream) + consumer (rolling KPIs, alerts)\n\nAirflow (daily, 2 AM):  data-quality gate --> train --> evaluate --> promote --> RAG refresh</pre>\n\n          <h4>Dataset</h4>\n          <p>AI4I 2020 Predictive Maintenance Dataset (UCI, CC BY 4.0): 10,000 records at a 3.39% failure rate, across five independent failure modes (tool wear, heat dissipation, power, overstrain, random). Raw signals include air and process temperature, rotational speed, torque, tool wear, and a product quality variant (L 50% / M 30% / H 20%).</p>\n\n          <h4>Feature Engineering</h4>\n          <p>16 features are derived from the 5 raw sensor readings so the physics of failure is explicit rather than latent. Representative examples:</p>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Derivation</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><code>temp_diff</code></td><td>Process temp &minus; Air temp</td><td>Heat dissipation indicator</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>power_W</code></td><td>Torque &times; (2&pi; &times; RPM / 60)</td><td>Power failure detection</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>wear_torque_product</code></td><td>Tool wear &times; Torque</td><td>Overstrain indicator</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>tool_wear_ratio</code></td><td>Tool wear / 240</td><td>Normalized wear proximity to failure</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>Results</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>F1</th><th>AUC-ROC</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Gradient Boosting</strong></td><td><strong>0.8548</strong></td><td><strong>0.9591</strong></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Random Forest</td><td>0.8387</td><td>0.9823</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Logistic Regression</td><td>0.3054</td><td>0.9455</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>Gradient Boosting was selected and registered in MLflow as the production model. A newly trained model must clear a 0.80 F1 threshold in the nightly pipeline before it can be promoted, so a bad batch never reaches production.</p>\n\n          <h4>Setup</h4>\n          <p>The whole system runs end to end with Docker Compose behind a Makefile:</p>\n          <pre>git clone https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/shopfloor-ai\ncd shopfloor-ai\ncp .env.example .env      # add your OPENAI_API_KEY\nmake setup                # install deps, download AI4I data, build images\nmake train                # train 3 models, log to MLflow, register the best\nmake up                   # start all 7 services\nmake ingest               # load 6 SOPs into ChromaDB for RAG</pre>\n          <p>Services come up at: dashboard (:8501), API docs (:8001/docs), MLflow (:5001), Airflow (:8080). A 15-test suite (<code>make test</code>) and GitHub Actions CI cover data loading, feature engineering, streaming, and SOP structure.</p>\n\n          <h4>Limitations and Future Work</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Synthetic dataset.</strong> AI4I is 10,000 synthetic records; a production deployment would integrate real MES/SCADA feeds and far larger volumes.</li>\n            <li><strong>Replayed streaming.</strong> The Kafka pipeline replays historical records rather than consuming a live feed; the architecture supports real connectors.</li>\n            <li><strong>Bundled SOPs.</strong> RAG answers depend on the 6 shipped documents; organization specific docs would improve retrieval relevance.</li>\n            <li><strong>Planned.</strong> SHAP explanations in the prediction API, multi model A/B testing in the DAG, WebSocket live dashboard updates, and AWS ECS deployment with S3 backed MLflow storage.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">The interesting engineering is everything around the model: a nightly retraining schedule with data quality gates, a registry that separates trained from deployed, a streaming layer that keeps predictions current, and a RAG assistant grounded in real SOPs. That is the difference between a model that scores well once and a system a plant could actually depend on.</p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "handoff: Supervisor Orchestration with Escalations That Have Deadlines", "meta": "Agent Orchestration &middot; Human in the Loop &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "212 tests &middot; zero dependencies &middot; unanswered page &rarr; safer substitute", "tags": ["Python", "Agent Orchestration", "Supervisor", "Human in the Loop", "Escalation Policy", "State Machine", "Audit Trail", "Zero Dependencies", "unittest", "CI/CD"], "body": "<div class='metric'>212 tests &middot; 11 behavioural scenarios &middot; zero dependencies &middot; unanswered page &rarr; safer substitute &middot; CI green on Python 3.11&ndash;3.13</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>Most human-in-the-loop is a modal dialog that blocks forever. The agent reaches something risky, calls confirm(), and waits. It does not say who is being asked, it does not say how long they have, and it has no answer at all for the case that actually happens at 3am, which is that nobody replies. An agent parked on an unanswered prompt is worse than one that never asked, because the incident is still burning and now nothing is watching it. handoff takes the position that escalation is a routing problem with tiers, skills, load, deadlines and a written down behaviour for silence, and builds the rest of the system around that.</p>\n\n<h4>The Sequel to comms-agent</h4>\n<p>comms-agent put a human-in-the-loop gate inside an agent graph: flag the low-confidence items, let a reviewer correct them, then report. That answers what happens when the reviewer shows up. handoff asks the next question, which is what happens when they do not. Same instinct, pushed into the case that actually breaks these systems in production.</p>\n\n<h4>Escalation as Policy, Not a Callback</h4>\n<p>The policy is a table keyed by risk, held as data in config/policy.yaml: which tiers may approve, in what order, how many seconds the first tier gets, how many signatures are required, and what happens on expiry. Because it is data it is reviewable in a pull request by somebody who does not read Python. Four classes of mistake are caught at load time rather than during an incident: a rule that requires approval without stating an expiry behaviour, a quorum larger than its own ladder, a ladder tier that no responder stands on, and a declared fallback that is missing, self referential, or not actually safer.</p>\n\n<h4>When Nobody Answers</h4>\n<p>An irreversible failover_database is proposed. The page goes to the on-call engineer holding the infrastructure skill, with 180 seconds to answer. Nobody answers. The system neither fails over nor sits still: it runs enable_read_only instead, which is the substitute the failover tool itself declares. Writes are rejected and checkout degrades, but no in-flight transaction is lost and a human can reverse it on arrival. The journal records that it happened because nobody answered, which is a different fact from a human deciding it was a good idea.</p>\n\n<h4>Disagreement Is a Second Trigger</h4>\n<p>Risk is not the only reason to involve a person. When the log analyst sees a high error rate and the metrics analyst sees healthy latency, both confidently, the hypothesis is marked contested rather than averaged into a clean answer the system does not actually have. Any action addressing a contested hypothesis has its effective risk raised one level, so a low-risk service restart that would normally run unattended stops and asks. Uncertainty about the diagnosis and danger in the fix are different problems with the same remedy, and one mechanism means nobody has to remember to wire up a second check.</p>\n\n<h4>What It Refuses To Do</h4>\n<p>The expiry behaviours are deny, safe_fallback, escalate and hold. There is deliberately no allow. Approving an action because nobody looked at it in time is not approval, it is the absence of a decision written down as though it were one, and a ladder whose terminal state can be \"ran it anyway\" is a delay mechanism wearing the costume of a control. A test asserts that the enum member does not exist, so adding it means deleting a test whose docstring explains why it should not.</p>\n\n<h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The cluster is simulated.</strong> It is a dataclass. Nothing in the project has talked to Kubernetes, a database, or a real pager.</li>\n<li><strong>Rules, not models.</strong> The five workers are deterministic with hand-written thresholds and no LLM is called anywhere. Whether the escalation layer holds up when the workers are non-deterministic is the open question this project does not answer.</li>\n<li><strong>No scheduler.</strong> The deadline tick is driven by the caller, which is exactly what makes every deadline testable at its precise boundary, and it also means a deployment needs something to call it on a timer that is not written here.</li>\n<li><strong>No notification channel.</strong> The journal exposes a subscribe hook where a pager would attach. Nothing attaches to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Static roster.</strong> Seven people in a YAML file, with no rotation schedule, timezones, or handover.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class='callout'>\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<p style='margin-bottom: 0;'>Agent orchestration where the supervision is the product rather than a wrapper around it: a supervisor that is the only component permitted to execute, workers handed a registry filtered to read-only tools so they cannot reach a mutating one by name, and an escalation layer with tiers, skills, load balancing, quorum, deadlines and a declared behaviour for silence. Built dependency-free so the whole suite runs on a bare interpreter in under a tenth of a second, and tested hard enough that the suite caught two design flaws during the build, one of which was a safety path that could never actually run.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "comms-agent: Agent Orchestration for Communications Monitoring", "meta": "Agent Orchestration &middot; LLM Systems &middot; Aug 2026", "badge": "stateful graph &middot; tool calling &middot; 9 of 24 flagged, 7 corrected via HITL gate", "tags": ["Python", "Agent Orchestration", "State Graph", "Tool Calling", "LLM", "Human in the Loop", "Reporting"], "body": "<div class='metric'>24 mention run &middot; 9 flagged low-confidence &middot; 7 corrected by human before reporting &middot; plan &rarr; classify &rarr; HITL gate &rarr; report</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>This is the automation layer that sits on top of a monitoring pipeline. Where media-pulse is the measurement system, comms-agent is the thing that runs it and produces the brief. It targets three qualifications that come up together: agent orchestration with planning and tool calling, AI-driven automation combined with human quality assurance, and responsible-AI practice through an explicit confidence gate and an audit trail.</p>\n\n<h4>The Agent</h4>\n<p>The flow is plan, ingest, classify, then a conditional review gate, then human review, then aggregate, then report. The gate is a conditional edge: if any mention came back below the confidence threshold it routes to human review, otherwise it skips straight to aggregation. That is the human-in-the-loop quality gate expressed as agent control flow. On the sample run the agent flags 9 of 24 mentions and a reviewer corrects 7 of them before anything is reported, so the numbers in the brief are the human-verified ones.</p>\n\n<h4>The Orchestrator</h4>\n<p>Underneath is a small state-graph engine with the pieces that matter in production agent frameworks: named nodes over a shared state, fixed and conditional edges, per-node retries, a max-step guard against infinite loops, and a complete execution trace. It is deliberately framework-free, and porting the same graph to a library like LangGraph is a mechanical change. Every tool call is recorded, so a run produces an auditable trace of exactly what the agent did.</p>\n\n<h4>The LLM</h4>\n<p>The agent depends only on a small LLM interface, so a deterministic mock model is the default, which keeps runs and tests reproducible offline. Swapping in a hosted model is a one-method change with no edits elsewhere, because the pipeline depends on the interface, not the provider.</p>\n\n<h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synthetic sample.</strong> It runs on the same fictional-brand corpus as media-pulse.</li>\n<li><strong>Mock model.</strong> The default LLM templates the report wording; a hosted model improves the phrasing, not the pipeline.</li>\n<li><strong>Simulated reviewer.</strong> The human review step is oracle backed in the demo, standing in for a real review UI in production.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class='callout'>\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<p style='margin-bottom: 0;'>Agent orchestration built from first principles: a stateful graph with planning, tool calling, conditional routing, retries, and a human-in-the-loop gate, wrapped around a real task and producing an auditable trace. It shows AI-driven automation that keeps a human on the uncertain tail rather than trusting the model blindly.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "Project Mark: Designing Deterministic Analytics Traps to Stress Test Frontier Models", "meta": "AI Evaluation &middot; Analytics Red Teaming &middot; Jul 2026", "badge": "4 verified statistical traps &middot; frontier-model evaluation", "tags": ["Python", "Pandas", "NumPy", "SciPy", "Statistical Inference", "Causal Reasoning", "Experiment Design", "LLM Evaluation", "Data Cleaning", "Reproducibility"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">5 datasets profiled &nbsp;|&nbsp; 4 trap families built and verified &nbsp;|&nbsp; deterministic golden solutions reproduced to the digit &nbsp;|&nbsp; CC licensed multi file corpora &nbsp;|&nbsp; frontier models solved the clean traps</div>\n\n          <h4>What The Project Was</h4>\n          <p>\n            The brief was unusually specific. Every submission had to be a product analytics question of the kind a technology company actually faces, analyzing how users behave inside a digital product to drive a product, growth, or monetization decision. Each had to resolve to a single deterministic recommendation that ten domain experts would independently agree on, be reconstructible entirely from the shipped files, and be hard enough that a panel of frontier models would fail it. The bar for a valid stump was concrete: across eight model responses, at least two of the first four and one of the second four had to land on a meaningfully different answer. Difficulty had to come from methodology and reasoning, never from vague wording or a trick definition.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>The Real Constraint: Fairness Plus Difficulty At The Same Time</h4>\n          <p>\n            The interesting tension is that a good task has to be simultaneously fair and hard. Fair means the correcting fact lives somewhere in the corpus, every file opens with standard tooling, and all defensible cleanings converge on the same answer. Hard means a strong analyst, or a strong model, still gets it wrong on the first pass. On top of that, the input files had to be real and appropriately licensed (public domain or Creative Commons only, verified per dataset), never model generated, six or more files across a variety of formats with several of them load bearing. Satisfying every one of those at once, on real data, is most of the work.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Traps I Built And Verified</h4>\n          <p>\n            Every trap below was implemented against real data and checked with code so the intended answer reproduced exactly before anything shipped. The pattern in each case is a headline number that is real and easy to compute but misleading, and a correcting analysis that a rushed solver skips.\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Composition shift (Simpson's paradox).</strong> A blended retention or conversion metric that declines quarter over quarter while every underlying segment is flat or improving, because the mix of segments moved. The naive read (the product is getting worse) is refuted by a segmentation the solver has to discover and join across files on a mismatched key.</li>\n            <li><strong>Outlier artifact in an A/B revenue test.</strong> On a real experiment dataset, one arm's revenue looked competitive, but 123 users (0.06 percent of the group) each paid a near identical anomalous amount and accounted for 89.4 percent of that arm's revenue, while the other arm had none. The correct call requires recognizing the cluster as non genuine, handling the zero inflated heavy tailed distribution properly (decompose conversion times value, use robust tests), and flipping the decision the raw averages imply.</li>\n            <li><strong>Confounded and circular metrics.</strong> A composite health score presented as independent corroboration that actually embeds the very metric under investigation, so citing it double counts one signal, plus correlation dressed up as causation on observational data.</li>\n            <li><strong>Mismatched population merges.</strong> A distractor file with similar looking columns that covers a different population entirely (different platform, window, or cohort), tempting a solver to merge it in and report a figure that does not apply to the group in question.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>The Final Task: An Engagement Feature Decision On Real Learning Platform Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            The most fully developed task used the Open University Learning Analytics Dataset (real virtual learning environment click logs, registrations, and outcomes for tens of thousands of students, released under CC BY). The business question: fund exactly one engagement initiative to raise course pass rates, either a richer per session experience or a nudge to return on more days. A shipped internal pre read recommended the per session option, backed by a real and reproducible correlation (total clicks versus passing at r = 0.56, with pass rate climbing from 0.3 percent to 91.9 percent across activity quintiles).\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            That correlation is the trap. Total activity confounds two distinct levers: how many days a student returns, and how much they do per session. Decomposing clicks into active days times clicks per active day and regressing both against passing shows the entire association lives in return frequency (standardized effect around 0.24, and about 0.34 with all students included) while per session intensity collapses to essentially zero once days present is controlled. The result held within every module and whether or not withdrawn students were included, so the deterministic answer is to fund return frequency, not intensity. I built two corroborating decoy artifacts pointing at the wrong lever, verified every figure reproduced from the raw files, and the task cleared the program's automated construction and determinism checks.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Why This Is A Happy Failure</h4>\n          <p>\n            It did not stump the models. In repeated live rollouts, strong frontier models independently rebuilt the analysis from the raw files, ran the frequency versus intensity decomposition, ignored the decoy pre reads, and reached the correct answer, often quoting the exact adjusted numbers. Hardening the framing and adding more authoritative decoys did not change the outcome. The reason is structural and worth stating plainly: the move that cracks the task, decompose a composite metric and test the parts, is a reflexive step for a capable analyst, so a frontier model does it whether or not the prompt signposts it.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            That is a genuinely useful finding, not a wasted effort. Stumping a frontier model on a clean, fair analytics problem requires a trap that sits outside a strong reasoner's default repertoire, which is a far higher bar than \"a smart person would need to be careful here.\" Watching capable models refuse a plausible but confounded headline artifact and reconstruct the correct causal story from first principles is a concrete, calibrated data point about where these systems are now strong, and it reshaped how I think about designing evaluations for them.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              Statistical rigor under adversarial conditions: constructing and reproducibly verifying deterministic traps (Simpson's paradox, outlier and confounding artifacts, correlation versus causation, population mismatches) on real, licensed, multi file data. It also shows the parts that are easy to undervalue, disciplined data provenance and licensing, golden solutions checked to the digit before shipping, and the willingness to report an honest negative result. The takeaway I care about most is the calibrated read on frontier model capability that only came from trying, and failing, to beat it fairly.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "media-pulse: Communications Measurement with Human-in-the-Loop QA", "meta": "Applied NLP &middot; Human in the Loop &middot; Aug 2026", "badge": "topic acc 0.88 &rarr; 0.96 with review &middot; sentiment 0.50 &rarr; 0.85 &middot; ECE 0.156", "tags": ["Python", "Scikit learn", "VADER", "TF-IDF", "Logistic Regression", "Streamlit", "Calibration (ECE)", "Human in the Loop", "Responsible AI"], "body": "<div class='metric'>120 sample mentions &middot; topic accuracy 0.88 &rarr; 0.96 after review &middot; sentiment 0.50 &rarr; 0.85 &middot; topic ECE 0.156 &middot; held out test n = 48</div>\n\n<h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n<p>Communications measurement roles ask for a specific mix at once: AI classification paired with human quality assurance, media and sentiment analytics, internal tooling for non technical teams, and responsible-AI practice. media-pulse is that full loop in one system, ingestion through reporting, rather than a single model in a notebook.</p>\n\n<h4>What It Does</h4>\n<p>Ingestion is pluggable: a bundled sample source ships with the project and an RSS adapter shows how a live feed drops into the same schema without changing anything downstream. Each mention is scored for sentiment (VADER) and topic (a TF-IDF and logistic-regression model), with a confidence attached to every prediction, plus message pull-through detection for pre approved key messages and a relevance score.</p>\n\n<h4>Human in the Loop</h4>\n<p>This is the core idea. Any prediction whose confidence falls below a threshold is routed to a review queue instead of being trusted. A reviewer confirms or corrects it, the corrected label overwrites the model output, and every action is written to an immutable audit log. On the sample corpus this lifts held out topic accuracy from 0.88 to 0.96 and sentiment accuracy from 0.50 to 0.85, and the dashboard shows the uplift directly. It is the same pattern as large citizen-science classification pipelines: the model does the bulk of the work and humans adjudicate the uncertain tail.</p>\n\n<h4>Responsible AI</h4>\n<p>Alongside the business metrics (coverage over time, sentiment mix, share of voice weighted by outlet reach, message pull-through), the system runs a quality review of the classifier itself: accuracy on a held out test set, expected calibration error so the confidence scores are honest, and an accuracy breakdown by outlet type that surfaces where the model is weakest.</p>\n\n<h4>Runs Anywhere</h4>\n<p>A four-tab Streamlit dashboard (overview, coverage, review queue, model QA) sits on top. The project falls back to bundled numpy and lexicon models when scikit-learn or VADER are not installed, so it runs offline and upgrades to the production models automatically once the requirements are installed.</p>\n\n<h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synthetic corpus.</strong> The data is a deterministic sample about a fictional brand, built to demonstrate the pipeline, not a live media feed.</li>\n<li><strong>Fallback models are weaker.</strong> The lexicon sentiment used offline is less accurate than a hosted model, which is part of why the human review uplift is large.</li>\n<li><strong>Single node.</strong> It runs on one machine, not at production stream volume.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class='callout'>\n<h4>What This Project Demonstrates</h4>\n<p style='margin-bottom: 0;'>One repository covering the qualifications that appear together in communications and analytics roles: AI-driven classification with human-in-the-loop QA, media and sentiment measurement, an internal-facing dashboard, an end to end data pipeline, and responsible-AI practice through calibration, subgroup review, and a full audit trail.</p>\n</div>"}, {"title": "Multi Horizon Geomagnetic Kp Index Prediction", "meta": "Deep Learning &middot; 2026", "badge": "45% better than baseline &middot; 96K+ hourly obs", "tags": ["LSTM", "TensorFlow", "Keras", "Time Series", "NASA OMNI2", "Multi Output", "Regularization"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">1h RMSE = 0.738 (45% better than baseline) &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1h MAE = 0.476 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1h R&sup2; = 0.690 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 96,000+ hourly observations</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Geomagnetic storms, caused by solar wind disturbances interacting with Earth's magnetosphere, are not abstract. The 1989 Quebec blackout cut power to six million people for nine hours. The 2003 Halloween storms damaged satellites and disrupted aviation. The economic exposure of severe storms is in the billions of dollars. Operators need lead time, ideally six to twelve hours, to safe satellites, derate transformers, and reroute aviation away from polar GPS dead zones. The Kp index (a 0 to 9 quasi logarithmic scale measured every three hours) is the standard operational trigger. Anything that improves Kp forecasting at multiple horizons translates directly into action lead time.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders and Why Multi Horizon Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Different operators need different lead times. Satellite mission controllers need 6 to 12 hour notice to plan orbital maneuvers and power down sensitive instruments. National grid operators need a few hours to derate transformers ahead of geomagnetically induced currents. GPS dependent industries (aviation, surveying, autonomous vehicles, precision agriculture) act on shorter horizons. The reason this model predicts all four horizons in one forward pass instead of training four separate models is that a single, internally consistent forecast across the full operational range is far more useful than four separate forecasts whose predictions might silently disagree.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            Source: NASA's OMNI2 high resolution database, hourly measurements of solar wind and interplanetary magnetic field parameters. Eleven years of coverage (January 2013 to December 2024), totaling 96,552 hourly records. The window spans the declining phase of solar cycle 24, the 2019 to 2020 solar minimum, and the rising phase of solar cycle 25, giving the model exposure to both quiet and active regimes.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            From the original 55 parameters I selected 15 features grounded in physics literature and correlation analysis against Kp. Feature importance fell into clear physical categories:\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-000.jpg\" alt=\"Pearson correlation of 15 selected features with Kp, grouped by category\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 1. PEARSON CORRELATION OF SELECTED FEATURES WITH KP, GROUPED BY IMF, SOLAR WIND, DERIVED, AND GEOMAGNETIC INDICES</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <p>\n            The strongest predictor was ap_Index (r = 0.825), followed by auroral electrojet indices (AE r = 0.732, AL r = -0.663, AU r = 0.690). IMF_Bz_GSM showed weaker correlation than expected (r = -0.238), reflecting that the dataset includes all periods rather than just storm windows.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Architecture</h4>\n          <p>\n            Two layer LSTM with the following structure:\n          </p>\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Input sequence length</td><td>24 hours (24 timesteps &times; 15 features)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Output</td><td>4 predictions (Kp_1h, Kp_3h, Kp_6h, Kp_12h)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Loss function</td><td>Mean Squared Error</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Optimizer</td><td>Adam, initial learning rate 0.001</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Batch size</td><td>64 sequences</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Epochs</td><td>Maximum 50, early stopping at epoch 39</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Normalization</td><td>StandardScaler on all features</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <p>\n            Chronological split (50% train / 16.6% validation / 16.6% test) with 8,761 hour temporal gaps between sets to prevent leakage, since this is a time series problem where the sequential boundary matters. Training converged cleanly with the best validation loss at epoch 39, after which validation loss plateaued and training kept improving (the textbook overfit signature, caught by early stopping).\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-001.jpg\" alt=\"Training and validation loss curves over 49 epochs\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 2. TRAINING AND VALIDATION LOSS OVER EPOCHS, WITH BEST EPOCH 39 MARKED</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Results by Horizon</h4>\n\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Horizon</th><th>RMSE</th><th>MAE</th><th>R&sup2;</th><th>Baseline RMSE</th><th>Improvement</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>1 hour</strong></td><td><strong>0.738</strong></td><td><strong>0.476</strong></td><td><strong>0.690</strong></td><td>1.34</td><td><strong>+44.9%</strong></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>3 hours</td><td>0.910</td><td>0.655</td><td>0.528</td><td>1.34</td><td>+32.1%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>6 hours</td><td>1.073</td><td>0.793</td><td>0.343</td><td>1.34</td><td>+19.9%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>12 hours</td><td>1.207</td><td>0.906</td><td>0.170</td><td>1.34</td><td>+9.9%</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-002.jpg\" alt=\"LSTM RMSE vs mean baseline RMSE for all four horizons\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 3. LSTM RMSE VS MEAN BASELINE RMSE ACROSS 1, 3, 6, AND 12 HOUR HORIZONS</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <p>\n            Performance degrades smoothly with horizon, which is the expected behavior for any forecasting system since the signal to noise ratio falls with lead time. The 1 hour horizon is the most operationally useful for short term tactical actions (GPS protection, immediate satellite ops), while the 6 and 12 hour horizons support strategic planning even with reduced confidence.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Behavior During Real Storm Events</h4>\n          <p>\n            The most useful diagnostic is whether the model can flag a storm coming, even imperfectly. Below is a 200 hour window from the test set covering a real storm event (red shaded zone, where actual Kp crosses the threshold of 5). All four horizons capture the storm onset, but with different lag and magnitude. The 1 hour forecast tracks actual Kp closely; the 12 hour forecast smooths the peak but still indicates elevated activity, which is exactly what an operator needs.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-003.jpg\" alt=\"Multi-horizon LSTM predictions vs actual Kp over a 200 hour test window\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 4. MULTI HORIZON LSTM PREDICTIONS VS ACTUAL KP OVER A 200 HOUR TEST WINDOW, STORM PERIOD HIGHLIGHTED IN RED</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Avoiding the Data Leakage Trap</h4>\n          <p>\n            Geomagnetic indices like ap, AE, and AL are strongly correlated with Kp because they all derive from magnetometer measurements responding to the same underlying phenomena. This creates real risk of leakage if the model accidentally uses concurrent index values to predict Kp. I verified that the indices are 3 hour averaged values (not instantaneous), that the 24 hour lookback window uses strictly historical values to predict future Kp, and that the 1 year temporal gaps between train, validation, and test sets prevent any contamination across the splits. Operational forecasting literature confirms that including these indices is standard practice rather than a methodological shortcut.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Operational Reading</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              The 1 hour forecast clears a useful operational bar: 45% better RMSE than naive mean prediction, and R&sup2; of 0.690 (the model explains roughly two thirds of variance in storm intensity). For longer horizons, the model degrades but stays informative, which is exactly the property a real operator needs: a single model that gives the immediate alarm and a softer signal further out, instead of four separate models that might silently disagree.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "The Perfect Lap: F1 Pit Stop Strategy Optimization", "meta": "Machine Learning &middot; Jul 2025 to Dec 2025", "badge": "2.78-lap MAE &middot; 250K+ records &middot; Streamlit deployed", "tags": ["Random Forest", "Python", "Scikit learn", "Streamlit", "SHAP", "Pandas", "CatBoost", "Extra Trees"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Test MAE = 2.78 laps &nbsp;|&nbsp; Test R&sup2; = 0.591 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 250,000+ records &nbsp;|&nbsp; 45 engineered features &nbsp;|&nbsp; Streamlit deployed</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Pit stop timing is one of the highest leverage decisions in motorsport. A miscall on lap selection can cost a team between two and five constructor championship positions over a season, translating to millions of dollars in lost prize money and sponsorship value. Race engineers currently make these calls in the moment with limited time, leaning on experience and real time telemetry. The premise of this project was simple: if the model can predict the optimal lap accurately enough, it becomes a live sanity check that the human strategist either validates or knowingly overrides, instead of a guess they make alone.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders and Value</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Race engineers</strong> get a data driven baseline against which to justify or override intuition on race day.</li>\n            <li><strong>Strategy directors</strong> get a tool that surfaces broad patterns across decades of races, supporting pre season strategic planning.</li>\n            <li><strong>Broadcast analysts and fantasy F1 platforms</strong> get credible strategy probabilities they can communicate to viewers.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Data and Cleaning</h4>\n          <p>\n            Source: Formula 1 World Championship dataset on Kaggle (1950 to 2020), 250,000+ records across thirteen interconnected tables (races, results, pit stops, drivers, constructors, lap times, qualifying, status codes, circuits, and more). A custom <code>track_features</code> table was added for tire degradation level and pit lane time loss per circuit, since these physical realities drive strategy more than the raw schedule data captures. The data was filtered to post 2000 races to focus on the modern tire era, since pre 2000 strategy was driven by fuel weight rather than tire compounds. The final modeling dataset is 14,000+ driver race rows with 45 engineered features.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            Cleaning rules: removed invalid pit lap records (below lap 1 or above lap 40 are data entry errors), stripped drivers with more than two pit stops per race (these signal damage or weather chaos rather than normal strategy), and dropped early DNFs so the training set reflects strategic behavior rather than mechanical failures.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Recovering Missing Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            Pit records before 2012 were sparse. A naive drop missing strategy would have shrunk the dataset from 10,000 rows to 3,000. I trained a dedicated Random Forest imputer that recovered roughly 70% of missing first pit laps using six predictors that are reliably present even in older races: driver ID, constructor ID, race year, starting grid position, total race laps, and circuit ID. Imputation MAE was about six laps on a held out validation set, which is tolerable given pit windows typically span ten to fifteen laps.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Models Compared</h4>\n          <p>\n            Four tree based regressors plus a decision tree baseline, identical splits and cross validation. Neural networks and SVMs were deliberately excluded because they are computationally heavier and consistently underperform tree ensembles on tabular data with this feature mix.\n          </p>\n\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Model</th><th>Train MAE</th><th>Test MAE</th><th>Train R&sup2;</th><th>Test R&sup2;</th><th>Status</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Random Forest</strong></td><td>1.007</td><td><strong>2.78</strong></td><td>0.943</td><td><strong>0.591</strong></td><td>Selected</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Extra Trees</td><td>~0</td><td>2.91</td><td>1.000</td><td>0.551</td><td>Overfits</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>CatBoost</td><td>2.540</td><td>3.02</td><td>0.702</td><td>0.523</td><td>2nd best</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Gradient Boosting</td><td>3.234</td><td>3.15</td><td>0.564</td><td>0.487</td><td>Weakest</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <p>\n            Random Forest landed at Test MAE 2.78 laps and Test R&sup2; 0.591, meaning predicted pit windows fall within roughly three laps of what teams actually did. Extra Trees showed near perfect training scores but worse generalization, the classic memorization signature. Gradient Boosting struggled with the noise introduced by safety cars and weather changes, since it prefers smoother data patterns. CatBoost would normally have a categorical handling advantage, but our pipeline pre encoded categorical variables for fair comparison, neutralizing that edge.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/f1_01.png\" alt=\"Feature importance comparison across four models\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 1. FEATURE IMPORTANCE ACROSS RANDOM FOREST, EXTRA TREES, GRADIENT BOOSTING, AND CATBOOST</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>What the Model Learned</h4>\n          <p>\n            SHAP analysis on the Random Forest model revealed that race structure features dominate the prediction. The top five contributors are total laps, planned pit stop count, year (proxying for regulation changes), round, and starting grid position. Driver and constructor identities mattered considerably less than F1 commentary tradition suggests.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/f1_03.png\" alt=\"SHAP beeswarm plot of feature impact on model output\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 2. SHAP BEESWARM SHOWING DIRECTION AND MAGNITUDE OF EACH FEATURE'S IMPACT ON PREDICTED PIT LAP</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Practical Implication</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              Pit timing is more a property of the physical system (circuit, race length, planned stops) than of the person behind the wheel. The practical instruction for engineers: adapt strategy to circuit and race length first, team history second, driver style third.\n            </p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h4>Deployment</h4>\n          <p>\n            The final Random Forest model was serialized and packaged into a Streamlit web application (<code>team_omega_rf_app.py</code>). Race engineers input live race context (circuit, grid position, planned stops, weather) and the app returns a predicted first pit stop lap in real time, alongside the SHAP feature importances that drove the prediction. Serialized model artifacts (<code>f1_random_forest_model.pkl</code>, <code>model_features.pkl</code>) live in the project repository.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Honest Limitations</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>No live race signals.</strong> Safety car deployments, sudden weather changes, and real time tire telemetry are absent. These dynamic elements drive real pit decisions and represent a genuine information gap between the static model and live race conditions.</li>\n            <li><strong>First stop only.</strong> The model predicts only the first pit lap, not full multi stop strategies. Subsequent stops depend on the first, creating interdependencies the current approach does not capture.</li>\n            <li><strong>No competitor model.</strong> Real strategy reacts to opponents (undercuts, overcuts). The model encodes neither opponent positions nor gap sizes.</li>\n            <li><strong>Regulation drift.</strong> F1 rules change often. Patterns from early 2000s races may not apply directly to current regulations, slightly diluting accuracy for contemporary strategy prediction.</li>\n          </ul>"}, {"title": "Managing July Energy Demand Under a Warming Climate", "meta": "Predictive Modeling &middot; Jan 2025 to May 2025", "badge": "+25.4% peak load at +5&deg;C &middot; 9.2% savings modeled", "tags": ["Random Forest", "XGBoost", "Linear Regression", "Python", "Climate Scenarios", "Residential Energy"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Random Forest R&sup2; = 0.48 &nbsp;|&nbsp; RMSE = 0.500 kWh &nbsp;|&nbsp; +25.4% projected peak hour load at +5&deg;C &nbsp;|&nbsp; 9.2% savings from 1&deg;C setpoint shift</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Residential energy is the part of the grid most exposed to climate shocks. When July temperatures rise, air conditioning load swings hard upward, peak hour demand spikes, and grid operators face a choice: build more generation (slow, expensive, capital intensive) or shift demand through pricing and behavioral signals (fast, cheap, but only effective if you know what works). This project gives eSC the second tool. We forecast residential consumption under a sustained warming scenario, then quantify which interventions recover the most demand without infrastructure investment.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Utility capacity planners</strong> get a defensible peak load estimate under temperature shock, instead of extrapolating last summer.</li>\n            <li><strong>Demand response program managers</strong> get a quantified savings estimate for the simplest possible intervention (a 1&deg;C cooling setpoint shift), useful for justifying smart thermostat rollouts.</li>\n            <li><strong>Regulators and ratepayer advocates</strong> get evidence that behavioral and efficiency programs can substitute meaningfully for new generation capacity.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three datasets joined on common keys: a household metadata file (square footage, occupants, wall insulation type, cooling setpoint), an hourly energy consumption file linked by <code>bldg_id</code>, and a weather file linked by <code>in.county</code>. A metadata file documented each column's semantics. The combined data was filtered to July only to focus on peak cooling season, then merged into one record per house per hour. A reusable merge function was written so the same pipeline can be applied to other months and seasons.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>What Drives Residential Consumption</h4>\n          <p>\n            EDA established four primary drivers of July energy usage:\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"figure-row\">\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_12.png\" alt=\"Energy usage vs dry bulb temperature\">\n              <figcaption>FIGURE 1. ENERGY USAGE INCREASES WITH OUTDOOR DRY BULB TEMPERATURE</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_09.png\" alt=\"Energy usage vs square footage\">\n              <figcaption>FIGURE 2. ENERGY USAGE SCALES NEAR LINEARLY WITH HOUSEHOLD SQUARE FOOTAGE</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"figure-row\">\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_11.png\" alt=\"Energy usage by number of occupants\">\n              <figcaption>FIGURE 3. ENERGY USAGE BY NUMBER OF OCCUPANTS</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_10.png\" alt=\"Energy usage by wall insulation type\">\n              <figcaption>FIGURE 4. ENERGY USAGE BY WALL INSULATION TYPE</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n\n          <p>\n            Outdoor temperature is the dominant driver. Square footage scales linearly with consumption. Occupant count matters but plateaus quickly. Wall insulation shows surprisingly modest variation across the housing stock, suggesting envelope upgrades have lower marginal return than HVAC and setpoint interventions on this dataset.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Models Compared</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three models trained on five features (dry bulb temperature, square footage, occupants, cooling setpoint, hour of day):\n          </p>\n\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Model</th><th>RMSE (kWh)</th><th>R&sup2;</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Linear Regression</td><td>0.583</td><td>0.29</td><td>Cannot capture non linear temperature response</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>XGBoost</td><td>0.503</td><td>0.47</td><td>Strong on non linear patterns, close second</td></tr>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Random Forest</strong></td><td><strong>0.500</strong></td><td><strong>0.48</strong></td><td>Selected for forecasting</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>The +5&deg;C Stress Test</h4>\n          <p>\n            Sustained warming scenarios were applied directly to the temperature feature and pushed through the trained model:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Average July hour, baseline</strong>: 1.09 kWh predicted.</li>\n            <li><strong>Average July hour, +5&deg;C</strong>: 1.25 kWh predicted (a 14.7% increase).</li>\n            <li><strong>Peak hour (9 PM) baseline</strong>: 81,600 kWh aggregate across the sample.</li>\n            <li><strong>Peak hour (9 PM) at +5&deg;C</strong>: 102,355 kWh aggregate, a 25.4% surge.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Policy Lever: 1&deg;C Setpoint Adjustment</h4>\n            <p>\n              Layering a 1&deg;C cooling setpoint increase during peak hours (16:00 to 21:00) on top of the +5&deg;C warming scenario produces:\n            </p>\n            <ul>\n              <li>Average usage at +5&deg;C: 1.30 kWh per hour</li>\n              <li>Average usage with +1&deg;C setpoint adjustment: 1.18 kWh per hour</li>\n              <li><strong>Average savings: 0.12 kWh per hour, or 9.2% of peak hour demand</strong></li>\n            </ul>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              The number is what makes the analysis actionable. A single degree of thermostat tolerance, applied across a customer base, recovers nearly one tenth of warming driven peak load without any infrastructure investment.\n            </p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h4>Recommendations to eSC</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Time of use pricing during peak hours.</strong> Shift discretionary demand off peak through price signals rather than capacity expansion.</li>\n            <li><strong>Smart thermostat rollout.</strong> Enables the 1&deg;C setpoint adjustment to happen automatically across the customer base instead of relying on individual behavior change.</li>\n            <li><strong>Modern HVAC incentives.</strong> Older units carry disproportionate cooling load; replacement programs targeted at the worst performers compound the setpoint benefit.</li>\n            <li><strong>Passive cooling design for new builds.</strong> Long term lever that compounds over the multi decade life of housing stock.</li>\n          </ul>"}, {"title": "Challenging \"Wealth Equals Health\": Education as the Hidden Lever", "meta": "Quantitative Data Storytelling &middot; Jan 2025 to May 2025", "badge": "180+ countries &middot; ANOVA F = 426.5 (p &lt; 2e-16)", "tags": ["R", "ggplot2", "Multiple Regression", "ANOVA", "Bayesian Inference", "Gapminder"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">180+ countries &nbsp;|&nbsp; Period: 2000 to 2020 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Combined BF = 1.65 &times; 10&#179;&#x2078;&#x2078; &nbsp;|&nbsp; ANOVA F = 426.5 (p &lt; 2e-16)</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            \"Richer countries are healthier\" is one of the most common framings in development discourse, and it has a real consequence: development funding tends to flow toward GDP growth as the master strategy, with health and education treated as downstream beneficiaries. This project was designed to be both academically rigorous and practically useful, with the explicit goal of producing evidence a community leader can use to argue for education investment in rural communities. The audience for the final report is intentionally non technical: adults, teenagers, and children who can act on the conclusion.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Research Questions</h4>\n          <ol>\n            <li>To what extent does income influence life expectancy across countries?</li>\n            <li>Can education serve as a more effective tool than income in reducing child mortality?</li>\n          </ol>\n\n          <h4>Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            Gapminder global dataset covering 180+ countries between 2000 and 2020. Variables:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>GDP per capita</strong> as the measure of national income, log transformed to address right skew from extreme high income outliers.</li>\n            <li><strong>Life expectancy</strong> as the primary health outcome, ranging from approximately 40 to 85 years.</li>\n            <li><strong>Child mortality</strong> as the most policy responsive health metric, ranging from fewer than 5 deaths per 1,000 births in high income countries to over 150 in low income countries.</li>\n            <li><strong>Mean years of schooling</strong> for adults aged 25 to 34, the most reliable cross country proxy for education access, ranging from 2 to 15 years.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Exploratory Visualizations</h4>\n\n          <div class=\"figure-row\">\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_02.png\" alt=\"GDP vs Life Expectancy scatter plot with regression line\">\n              <figcaption>FIGURE 1. LOG GDP VS LIFE EXPECTANCY, ALL COUNTRIES 2000 TO 2020</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_04.png\" alt=\"GDP vs Child Mortality scatter plot with regression line\">\n              <figcaption>FIGURE 2. LOG GDP VS CHILD MORTALITY, ALL COUNTRIES 2000 TO 2020</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_03.png\" alt=\"Education vs Child Mortality scatter plot showing strong negative relationship\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 3. MEAN YEARS OF SCHOOLING VS CHILD MORTALITY, SHOWING A STRONGER, TIGHTER NEGATIVE RELATIONSHIP THAN GDP</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Question 1: Does GDP Predict Life Expectancy?</h4>\n          <p>\n            Linear regression of life expectancy on log GDP per capita yielded a coefficient of <strong>17.6</strong> (p &lt; 2e-16), confirming a strong positive relationship. Bayesian regression returned a Bayes Factor of <strong>5.66 &times; 10&#178;&#185;&#x2078;</strong> in favor of GDP as a predictor, which is overwhelming evidence. The answer to the first question is yes, GDP matters.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Question 2: Does Education Beat GDP for Child Mortality?</h4>\n          <p>\n            Multiple regression of child mortality on both mean years of schooling and log GDP returned two significant negative coefficients:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Mean years of schooling</strong>: estimate = -0.76, p &lt; 2e-16</li>\n            <li><strong>Log GDP per capita</strong>: estimate = -74.14, p &lt; 2e-16</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p>\n            Bayesian model comparison returned a combined Bayes Factor of <strong>1.65 &times; 10&#178;&#x2078;&#x2078;</strong> in favor of the model that includes both predictors, an extraordinarily large number that translates to overwhelming evidence both predictors carry independent information about child mortality.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Confirmatory Tests</h4>\n          <p>\n            Welch's t test compared child mortality between high education and low education country groups:\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_09.png\" alt=\"Boxplot of child mortality by education group\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 4. CHILD MORTALITY BY EDUCATION GROUP (WELCH'S T TEST). MEAN DIFFERENCE &#8776; 57 DEATHS PER 1,000 BIRTHS, P &lt; 2.2E-16</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <p>\n            One way ANOVA on life expectancy by GDP group (Low, Medium, High) returned F = 426.5, p &lt; 2e-16:\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_10.png\" alt=\"Boxplot of life expectancy by GDP group\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 5. LIFE EXPECTANCY BY GDP GROUP (ANOVA, F = 426.5, P &lt; 2E-16)</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>The Practical Conclusion</h4>\n            <p>\n              Income matters, but it is not the lever development funders should pull first. Education is just as strong a predictor of child mortality as GDP is, and unlike GDP, education is something a rural community can act on directly through school access, teacher training, and maternal literacy programs.\n            </p>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              Each additional year of schooling is associated with a measurable drop in child deaths, regardless of country wealth. For a community leader, the evidence based call to action is clear: keep children (especially girls) in school through secondary education, combine schooling with practical instruction on hygiene, nutrition, vaccination, and early childhood care, and advocate for school infrastructure investment. The data shows these efforts have life saving consequences in a way that simply waiting for GDP growth does not.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "Uber Data Engineering Pipeline", "meta": "Data Engineering &middot; Jul 2024 to Dec 2024", "badge": "End-to-end GCP pipeline &middot; star schema &middot; BigQuery", "tags": ["GCP", "BigQuery", "Power BI", "SQL", "Python", "ETL", "Star Schema"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">End to end cloud pipeline &nbsp;|&nbsp; Star schema modeling &nbsp;|&nbsp; Cloud Storage + Compute Engine + BigQuery &nbsp;|&nbsp; Interactive BI dashboard</div>\n\n          <h4>Objective</h4>\n          <p>\n            Most data engineering exercises stop at a notebook. I wanted to do the full lifecycle in one project: ingestion, storage, transformation, modeling, querying, and visualization, end to end on a real cloud stack. The deliverable was not just a model or a chart, but the entire path from raw data file to stakeholder ready dashboard.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Architecture</h4>\n          <p>\n            Raw NYC taxi trip data was modeled into a star schema with fact and dimension tables in Lucid Charts, with explicit primary and foreign key relationships. Infrastructure was deployed on Google Cloud Platform with three layers:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Storage layer.</strong> Cloud Storage as the data lake, holding raw and intermediate Parquet files.</li>\n            <li><strong>Compute layer.</strong> Compute Engine VMs running Python ETL jobs that read from Cloud Storage, transform with Pandas and NumPy, and write back as cleaned Parquet.</li>\n            <li><strong>Analytics layer.</strong> BigQuery for SQL analytics at scale, with the cleaned data loaded as partitioned tables for efficient time range queries.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Analysis and Presentation</h4>\n          <p>\n            Exploratory analysis in Jupyter surfaced patterns in trip distance distribution, fare structure, revenue concentration, and service utilization across boroughs and times of day. The findings then fed an interactive Power BI dashboard with geographical heat maps, time series views, and slicers for stakeholder driven exploration.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Lesson</h4>\n          <p>\n            The real lesson was less about any individual tool and more about what moving from local CSV files to distributed cloud storage and compute fundamentally changes about how you think. When data lives in object storage and compute is elastic, scaling up by an order of magnitude becomes a configuration change rather than a bottleneck. That mindset shift is the actual deliverable.\n          </p>"}, {"title": "Travel Itinerary Management System", "meta": "Database Engineering &middot; Jan 2025 to May 2025", "badge": "12-entity normalized schema &middot; full itinerary in one call", "tags": ["SQL", "Data Modeling", "Stored Procedures", "Triggers", "User Defined Functions", "ERD Design", "3NF"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">12 normalized entities &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3 stored procedures &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2 triggers &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1 user defined function &nbsp;|&nbsp; Single procedure call generates full itinerary</div>\n\n          <h4>Why This Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Travel apps rely on enforcing rules consistently across every access path: an activity cannot have negative cost, a hotel must belong to a real destination, an itinerary cost should always equal the sum of its components. The naive approach puts those rules in application code, where each new feature or platform reintroduces them, badly. The mature approach puts the rules in the database itself, so any client (web, mobile, batch import, ad hoc SQL) gets the same guarantees automatically. This project demonstrates the second approach.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Travelers</strong> creating a trip get a single endpoint to call that returns a complete itinerary, with all the validation already done.</li>\n            <li><strong>Application developers</strong> get to stop writing defensive code in three languages to enforce business rules.</li>\n            <li><strong>Data analysts</strong> querying the database directly get clean, consistent data because the integrity rules apply even to direct SQL access.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Entity Relationship Design</h4>\n          <p>\n            Twelve interconnected entities in third normal form with full referential integrity. The core entity is <code>itinerary</code>, which links to destinations, activities, accommodations, transportation, seasonal information, and tags through explicit foreign keys.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/travel_03.png\" alt=\"Logical ERD showing all 12 tables with primary keys, foreign keys, and relationships\">\n            <figcaption>FIGURE 1. LOGICAL ERD WITH ALL 12 TABLES, PRIMARY KEYS, FOREIGN KEYS, AND CARDINALITY RELATIONSHIPS</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Stored Procedures</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three procedures cover the operations a travel app actually performs:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>sp_add_full_itinerary_single</code>: Inserts a complete itinerary (activity, transport, accommodation, season info, tag) in a single atomic call. Replaces what would otherwise be five separate inserts plus consistency checks at the application layer.</li>\n            <li><code>sp_get_full_itinerary</code>: Retrieves all linked details of an itinerary by ID, with joins handled inside the database.</li>\n            <li><code>sp_get_itineraries_for_destination</code>: Lists all itineraries available under a selected destination, the most common browse query.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Triggers for Data Integrity</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>trg_check_activity_duration</code>: Rejects any activity insert or update where duration is zero or negative. Protects against a category of UI bugs and data import errors that would otherwise silently corrupt the cost calculations downstream.</li>\n            <li><code>trg_check_activity_cost</code>: Rejects negative cost entries, which usually indicate a typo or unit conversion error rather than a genuine refund.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Function for Reusable Logic</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>ufn_estimate_total_activity_time</code>: Returns the total time a traveler will spend on all activities in a given itinerary. Used in views, filters, and ranking interfaces so itineraries can be sorted or filtered by total activity duration without recomputing the math each time.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>User Story Worked Through End to End</h4>\n          <p>\n            <em>\"As a traveler, I want to create a trip by selecting options, so I can generate a full itinerary.\"</em>\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            Implementation: the client passes destination, activity, transport, accommodation, season, and tag to <code>sp_add_full_itinerary_single</code>. The procedure inserts each child record, links them through the itinerary table, runs the duration and cost triggers, and returns the new <code>itinerary_id</code>. One round trip from the client, full validation, atomic on the database side.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Design Philosophy</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              Proper database design absorbs complex business rules at the data layer, reducing application complexity and guaranteeing integrity across every read and write path. When the database enforces invariants, you stop writing defensive code in multiple languages to protect them, and you stop debugging the same off by one bug five separate times for five separate clients.\n            </p>\n          </div>"}, {"title": "Digit Recognition Neural Network from Scratch", "meta": "Deep Learning &middot; Jan 2024 to May 2024", "badge": "94% MNIST accuracy &middot; built from scratch, no frameworks", "tags": ["Python", "NumPy", "Neural Networks", "Backpropagation", "From Scratch"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">94% test accuracy on MNIST &nbsp;|&nbsp; No ML frameworks &nbsp;|&nbsp; Manual forward and backward passes</div>\n\n          <h4>Why Build from Scratch</h4>\n          <p>\n            Frameworks abstract exactly the parts of a neural network that are most useful to understand: the matrix shapes flowing forward, the gradient math flowing backward, and the way optimization actually changes weights. Building all three by hand once gives you a working mental model for the rest of your career. When a production model later silently fails, you reason about gradient flow rather than swapping layers and hoping.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Implementation</h4>\n          <p>\n            Forward propagation was implemented as explicit weighted sums and activation functions: sigmoid for hidden layers in the first iteration, then ReLU once vanishing gradients became visible, with softmax at the output for the ten class classification. Backpropagation was coded as gradient computation via the chain rule, layer by layer, with explicit derivative formulas for each activation function. A mini batch gradient descent optimization loop handled training, with tunable learning rate, batch size, and network depth.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Tuning</h4>\n          <p>\n            Hyperparameter tuning was iterative and manual, which is exactly the point of this kind of exercise. Learning rate too high and the loss bounced. Too low and training stalled. Network too shallow and the model could not capture digit structure. Too deep without proper initialization and gradients vanished. Working through these failures by hand is how you learn what each knob actually controls.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Result</h4>\n          <p>\n            Final accuracy of 94% on the held out MNIST test set. Not state of the art, but the goal was understanding rather than benchmark chasing. The exercise made it intuitive why deeper networks, non linear activations, and proper initialization matter, which is the kind of grounding that makes debugging production models much easier later.\n          </p>"}, {"title": "Real Time Big Data Inflow: Communication and Computation Delay Analysis", "meta": "Research &middot; Jan 2024 to Dec 2024", "badge": "40&ndash;60% latency reduction &middot; 6-dimension comparison", "tags": ["IoT", "Edge Computing", "Fog Computing", "Cloud Computing", "Stream Architecture"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">40 to 60% latency reduction potential &nbsp;|&nbsp; 12+ primary sources reviewed &nbsp;|&nbsp; Comparative analysis across six dimensions</div>\n\n          <h4>Argument</h4>\n          <p>\n            The implicit default in most enterprise architectures is to send everything to the cloud. For real time systems, that default is wrong. The interesting design work is deciding which computations belong near the sensor (where latency is cheap but durability is fragile and storage is limited) and which belong in the data center (where storage is durable and compute is elastic but round trips cost real time).\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Approach</h4>\n          <p>\n            The paper reviews twelve primary sources on stream processing, edge deployment patterns, and latency optimization in distributed systems. Each source is summarized for its proposed architecture and headline findings. From the literature, I propose a tiered hybrid architecture that pushes time critical computation toward the edge while retaining cloud tiers for durability, batch analytics, and global state.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Comparative Analysis</h4>\n          <p>\n            Cloud only and tiered architectures are compared across six dimensions: scalability, reliability, bandwidth usage, privacy and security, cost effectiveness, and end to end latency. The tiered architecture wins on latency (40 to 60% reduction for appropriately partitioned workloads), bandwidth (less raw data crosses the WAN), and privacy (sensitive data can be processed locally), while paying a small price in operational complexity and consistency guarantees.\n          </p>"}];
